      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We can use the formula for finding the exponent of a prime $p$ in $n!$ which is given by:\n$$\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^k} \\right\\rfloor$$\nIn this case, we want to find the exponent of $3$ in $24!$, so we plug in $n=24$ and $p=3$ into the formula:\n$$\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3^k} \\right\\rfloor$$\nWe can simplify this sum by finding the values of $k$ for which the floor function is non-zero.  For $k=1$, we have:\n$$\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 8 \\right\\rfloor = 8$$\nFor $k=2$, we have:\n$$\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{9} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 2 \\right\\rfloor = 2$$\nFor $k=3$, we have:\n$$\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{27} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 0 \\right\\rfloor = 0$$\nSince the floor function becomes zero after $k=3$, we stop here and add up the values of $k$ for which the floor function is non-zero:\n$$8 + 2 + 0 = 10$$\nTherefore, the exponent of $3$ in $24!$ is $\\boxed{10}$.The answer is: 10",
