      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\), we need to determine how many times 3 appears as a factor in the numbers from 1 to 24. This is done by counting the multiples of 3, 9, 27 (if any), and so on, up to the largest power of 3 that is less than or equal to 24.\n\nWe start by counting the multiples of 3:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 8\n\\]\nThere are 8 multiples of 3 in the range from 1 to 24. Next, we count the multiples of \\(3^2 = 9\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{9} \\right\\rfloor = 2\n\\]\nThere are 2 multiples of 9 in the range from 1 to 24. Finally, we count the multiples of \\(3^3 = 27\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{27} \\right\\rfloor = 0\n\\]\nThere are no multiples of 27 in the range from 1 to 24.\n\nAdding these counts together gives the total exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\):\n\\[\n8 + 2 + 0 = 10\n\\]\nTherefore, the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
